# react-i18next / i18n

When adding or changing UI text, copy, labels, messages, or localization setup.

## Version source of truth

1. Read `react-i18next` and `i18next` from `@package.json`.
2. Use official docs: `https://react.i18next.com/` (and linked i18next docs when needed) for the installed majors (baseline hint: `react-i18next` **18**, `i18next` **27**).
3. Before non-trivial i18n setup or API choices: open the docs — do not invent patterns from older majors.
4. Cite concrete doc page URLs when recommending hooks, providers, or resource structure.

## Localization policy (even with one language)

The app may ship with a single locale for now, but **all user-facing strings must still go through i18n** so new languages can be added without rewriting UI.

- Do not hardcode user-visible text in components (`Text`, buttons, titles, placeholders, alerts, empty states, errors shown to users).
- Add keys to the locale resource files and render via `useTranslation` / `t(...)` (or project equivalents once set up).
- Prefer stable, namespaced keys (e.g. `screen.home.title`) over free-form English as keys.
- Interpolation and plurals: use i18next features from the docs, not string concatenation in components.
- Developer-only / non-UI strings (logs, analytics event names, test ids) may stay outside i18n.

## Preferences

- Follow existing i18n setup in this project when present; extend it instead of introducing a second library.
- If docs and memory conflict, the official react-i18next / i18next docs for this major win.
